# Mobile Platform Overview & Ecosystem

Our mobile engineering platform connects the capabilities teams need to **build, test, secure, deliver, and support mobile applications**. It brings together engineering standards, shared app foundations, development tools, automated pipelines, device testing, security assessment, observability, and distribution.

Start with the big picture here. Then follow the linked onboarding pages and the dedicated engineering sections for implementation instructions.

> **Confidential — internal use only.** Keep company source, architecture, configuration, and operational evidence within approved systems. Never include credentials, private artifact URLs, or sensitive user data in examples, screenshots, support requests, or AI prompts.

## 1. What the platform provides

| Capability | What it gives your team | When you use it |
| :--- | :--- | :--- |
| **Architecture and platform standards** | Clear boundaries for presentation, business rules, data access, state, and asynchronous work. | Designing and reviewing a feature. |
| **Shared foundations and SDKs** | Reusable application capabilities and documented integration patterns. | Building consistent experiences without duplicating platform infrastructure. |
| **Developer tools and GenAI guidance** | Approved workflows, project rules, and controlled assistance for authoring code and tests. | During local implementation and investigation. |
| **Testing and coverage** | Behavioral checks, coverage requirements, and platform-specific verification. | While implementing a change and before integration. |
| **Danger.js** | Automated feedback on configured PR conventions and metadata. | Preparing a pull request for review. |
| **Bitrise** | Governed build workflows, automated checks, artifact production, and delivery integration. | Validating changes and producing distributable builds. |
| **Sauce Labs** | Real-device execution and diagnostic evidence for supported mobile test frameworks. | Validating selected journeys across the approved device matrix. |
| **NowSecure** | Automated mobile security assessment and evidence for security decisions. | Evaluating artifacts at the policy-defined gates. |
| **Datadog** | Mobile experience, error, crash, and performance telemetry, with backend correlation where configured. | Verifying instrumentation and investigating application behavior. |
| **Distribution services** | Controlled delivery to enterprise users, testers, and store audiences. | Making a verified application version available to its intended users. |

These capabilities complement one another. A passing PR check does not replace tests; a successful build does not prove distribution; telemetry does not replace security assessment.

## 2. Understand how the ecosystem connects

The delivery path operates on source, test suites, and artifacts. The runtime path begins when users run the installed app.

<MermaidZoom title="Engineering ecosystem: from a reviewed change to an observable application">

```mermaid
sequenceDiagram
    autonumber
    actor DEV as Developer
    participant GIT as Source and PR Review
    participant CI as Bitrise
    participant SAUCE as Sauce Labs
    participant SEC as NowSecure
    participant DIST as Distribution
    actor USER as User
    participant APP as Mobile App
    participant OBS as Datadog

    DEV->>GIT: Submit feature and reviewed tests
    GIT->>CI: Trigger configured validation workflow
    CI->>CI: Run required checks and build artifacts
    opt Device testing required by the workflow
        CI->>SAUCE: Dispatch supported app and test packages
        SAUCE-->>CI: Return test outcomes and diagnostic evidence
    end
    opt Security assessment required by policy
        CI->>SEC: Submit supported artifact for assessment
        SEC-->>CI: Return completed assessment and gate evidence
    end
    Note over CI,DIST: Applicable checks and release authorization must be satisfied
