```

</MermaidZoom>

This diagram explains responsibilities, not a universal execution order. The approved workflow defines test packaging, concurrency, assessment placement, triggers, and approvals. A new application's governed enrollment happens before its normal build workflows can be used.

## 3. Build with the supported application stacks

> **Preferred stack: React Native.** React Native with TypeScript and Redux Toolkit is our strongly preferred starting point for new mobile applications. Evaluate it first. Choose native Android or iOS when documented requirements and integration evidence justify that decision with the responsible team.

For an existing app, follow its established stack unless a migration is explicitly agreed.

| Stack | What developers work with | What to understand |
| :--- | :--- | :--- |
| **React Native** | TypeScript, React Native components, Redux Toolkit where Redux state is needed, and native Android/iOS projects. | Shared feature code still requires platform-specific permissions, native dependencies, builds, and testing. |
| **Native Android** | Kotlin and the project's established Android UI and data technologies. | Android lifecycle, device integration, supported OS behavior, and native build configuration. |
| **Native iOS** | Swift and the project's established Apple UI and data technologies. | Apple lifecycle, concurrency, device capabilities, and native build configuration. |

### Make a new stack decision with evidence

Confirm target devices, OS versions, native SDK compatibility, accessibility, performance, team skills, and supported delivery templates. Prototype uncertain integrations on representative hardware.

Hardware integration does not automatically exclude React Native. Likewise, shared code does not guarantee that every capability or UI behavior will be identical across platforms. Avoid fixed code-sharing percentages and unsupported speed or performance claims.

Record the chosen stack, reasons, alternatives, constraints, integration evidence, and reviewers in the team's decision record.

## 4. Reuse application foundations

Shared foundations provide consistent behavior across features. Platforms & SDKs contains the platform-specific examples and supported contracts.

| Foundation | What developers should look for |
| :--- | :--- |
| **Design system and theming** | Reusable visual tokens, supported components, light/dark behavior, and accessible presentation. |
| **Authentication and session integration** | Approved sign-in, session handling, and account transitions. Confirm the supported integration for the assigned application. |
| **Secure storage** | Appropriate handling of permitted credentials and sensitive local data through approved abstractions. |
| **Localization** | Resource organization, locale selection, fallback, and platform behavior. |
| **Notifications and navigation** | Registration, receipt handling, validated payloads, and authorized destination routing. |
| **Branding** | Supported assets, application identity, and platform-specific branding behavior. |
| **Resilience and configuration** | Documented timeouts, failure handling, circuit-breaker policy where appropriate, and safe feature-flag defaults. |
| **Logout and cleanup** | Coordinated session invalidation, in-flight work, account-scoped storage, and cleanup verification. |

Confirm the actual SDK, package, version, and API before integrating. A capability described here is not a claim that one universal package implements it identically on all three stacks.

**Your responsibility:** use established contracts, preserve lifecycle and account boundaries, and add tests for the behavior your feature depends on.

## 5. Author changes with approved tools

Use the IDE appropriate to the project and approved tooling for formatting, analysis, debugging, and source control. GenAI & Developer Tools provides maintained rules and safe authoring workflows.

AI can help draft code, tests, and explanations from approved context. Developers remain responsible for contract accuracy, security, test quality, and the final change.

The agreed testing boundary is explicit: **AI-assisted generation happens during authoring; CI executes committed, human-reviewed tests without calling an LLM to generate them during the run.** This boundary alone does not make a pipeline offline or hermetic.

Do not provide secrets or unapproved company material to an assistant. Follow the company's approved tool and data-handling policy.

## 6. Validate behavior with tests and Sauce Labs

Testing starts locally, before a PR or release. Use focused tests for business logic, state transitions, adapters, and UI behavior, then broader integration and device checks where required.

**Coverage requirement:** the documentation requires 100% within the explicitly defined coverage scope. Testing & Quality defines platform-specific metrics, reporting, and enforcement. Every production file must be accounted for; a percentage does not prove that every behavior is correct.

### What Sauce Labs adds

Sauce Labs supplies real-device execution for the supported framework and device configuration. The testing documentation covers Espresso, XCUITest, and Appium/WebdriverIO approaches as applicable to the project.

| Developer need | Sauce Labs contribution |
| :--- | :--- |
| **Check target-device behavior** | Execute selected tests on the approved physical-device matrix. |
| **Investigate a failing journey** | Review available job logs, screenshots, recordings, and framework reports. |
| **Compare platform behavior** | Verify Android and iOS outcomes independently. |
| **Provide review evidence** | Associate the source revision, app build, test suite, device configuration, and job outcome. |

Native test runners and Appium suites have different packaging and execution contracts. Upload identifiers, application references, and job IDs must not be treated as interchangeable.

**Your responsibility:** maintain meaningful assertions, stable locators, suitable synchronization, isolated test data, and reproducible failures. Device-cloud access does not eliminate flaky tests or replace unit tests.

Detailed Sauce Labs setup and troubleshooting live in Testing & Quality. CI/CD explains the approved invocation and how results block or permit progression.

## 7. Automate builds and delivery with Bitrise

Bitrise runs the approved mobile workflows. Depending on the route and policy, these workflows resolve dependencies, run checks, build native artifacts, integrate testing and assessment, and deliver eligible outputs.

### Enroll once, then use the established app

For a new application, developers submit a **secret-free declarative enrollment request** to the platform configuration repository. Validation and review precede protected promotion. Authorized platform automation performs the live provisioning.

Developers do not manually create Bitrise apps or paste production signing credentials into the web UI as an onboarding shortcut. Existing applications reuse their enrolled resources.

### Understand the governed route names

| Route | Intended purpose |
| :--- | :--- |
| `qa_android` | Android build and private QA delivery through its configured destination. |
| `qa_ios` | iOS build and private QA delivery through its configured destination. |
| `uat_android` | Android build and delivery for the approved UAT audience. |
| `uat_ios` | iOS build and delivery for the approved UAT audience. |

The approved platform template determines route applicability. Native-only apps must not report unsupported routes as successful. Each applicable route needs its own evidence.

PR validation is separate from these delivery routes. Passing QA does not establish UAT publication, and these route names do not imply public production-store release.

**Your responsibility:** identify the exact workflow and source revision, inspect failed stages, and report outcomes accurately. Never repeat enrollment or an external write blindly after an unknown result; follow the safe-recovery process.

## 8. Understand PR and security gates

### Danger.js: review conventions

Danger.js provides automated feedback on the configured PR rules, such as description quality, assignees, requested reviewers, and draft/WIP markers. Actual rules and blocking behavior depend on the repository configuration and branch protection.

Danger does not inherently execute lint, measure coverage, assess security, or prove functional correctness. Those checks need their own configured tools and required statuses.

### NowSecure: artifact security assessment

NowSecure provides mobile security assessment and reports for supported artifacts and configured tests. Submission, completed assessment, and gate evaluation are separate outcomes.

Security policy defines blocking severity, assessment completeness, and approved exceptions. A passing assessment does not certify the app free of vulnerabilities. Assessment is not automatic binary hardening, and findings are not limited to CVEs.

**Your responsibility:** inspect actionable findings, coordinate remediation with the appropriate owner, and preserve evidence. Do not bypass a security gate or change a threshold to obtain a passing build.

## 9. Observe application behavior with Datadog

Datadog helps teams understand what happens after an app runs on a device. Instrumentation must be configured, verified, and associated with the correct environment and release.

| Capability | What it helps answer |
| :--- | :--- |
| **Real User Monitoring (RUM)** | Which views, actions, resources, and errors are associated with a user journey? |
| **Crash reporting** | Where did the app fail, and is the release's symbol or mapping information available to interpret the stack? |
| **Performance monitoring** | Which supported measurements show slow startup, interaction, rendering, or resource access? |
| **Backend correlation** | Can an instrumented mobile request be linked to backend traces through the configured propagation and sampling policy? |
| **Dashboards and alerts** | Is a release showing a regression, and which team needs to investigate? |

Correlation depends on client and backend instrumentation, allowed hosts, propagation, and sampling. It does not guarantee that every session includes a complete backend trace.

Observability contains separate Android, iOS, and React Native integration instructions, official Datadog references, symbol-upload requirements, verification, and troubleshooting. Session Replay requires its own approval and privacy design; it is not assumed enabled by installing RUM.

**Your responsibility:** emit approved, useful attributes and verify collection without disclosing sensitive data. Sampling is not masking, and hashed identifiers are not automatically anonymous.

## 10. Deliver to the intended audience

App Store & Distribution explains destination access, application records, signing requirements, test audiences, submission, review, and release verification. CI/CD explains how approved automation interacts with those destinations.

| Destination | What to distinguish |
| :--- | :--- |
| **Enterprise App Management** | Artifact ingestion, applicable distribution signing, version enablement, audience assignment, and exact-version visibility. |
| **Google Play** | Internal testing and other approved tracks versus public production release. |
| **App Store Connect and TestFlight** | Upload, processing, tester availability, App Review, and public release are distinct states. |

A binary can build successfully and still fail downstream signing, processing, assignment, or publication. Confirm the intended version and audience at the destination rather than inferring success from CI compilation.

## 11. Know which source and configuration to change

| Repository or resource | Purpose |
| :--- | :--- |
| **Application source** | Feature code, tests, dependency locks, and application configuration. |
| **Approved application template** | Generates a new application. The template root is not the generated app. |
| **Platform configuration** | Declarative enrollment requests and their review history. |
| **Provisioning engine** | Validation and live-apply implementation maintained by the responsible platform owners. |
| **Workflow catalog** | Approved workflow definitions and versions. |
| **Shared SDK source** | Reusable implementations and integration contracts. |

A source commit identifies the app code; a configuration merge commit identifies a configuration change. Record the relevant identities separately when investigating an enrollment or build.

Historical workshop requests, template versions, and successful routes are examples of one execution—not reusable enrollment requests or proof of current platform health.

## 12. Follow the complete developer journey

<MermaidZoom title="Developer journey: prepare, implement, verify, deliver, and support">

```mermaid
sequenceDiagram
    autonumber
    actor DEV as Developer
    participant TEAM as Application Team
    participant LOCAL as Local Workstation
    participant REVIEW as Review and CI
    participant RELEASE as Release Process
    participant OPS as Support and Observability

    DEV->>TEAM: Confirm app, owner, stack, and first task
    DEV->>LOCAL: Prepare access and supported toolchain
    DEV->>LOCAL: Run app and implement focused change
    DEV->>LOCAL: Add tests and privacy-safe instrumentation
    DEV->>REVIEW: Submit source and verification evidence
    REVIEW-->>DEV: Return feedback and current-revision results
    DEV->>REVIEW: Address feedback and satisfy required checks
    REVIEW->>RELEASE: Provide eligible revision and artifacts
